## Quick Take‑away

| Feature | What it represents | Typical use case |
|---------|--------------------|------------------|
| `std::optional<T>` | *Maybe‑a‑value*: an object that **may or may not contain a `T`**. | “Maybe the user gave me a value” – a function that can fail to produce a value, optional config values, lazy evaluation. |
| `std::variant<T1, T2, …>` | A **sum type**: it always contains **exactly one** of the listed alternatives. | A function that can return several different result types (e.g. `int` *or* an error message), an event system with many event payloads, a “discriminated union” pattern. |

Both are *type‑safe* alternatives to raw pointers, sentinel values, or unions, and they live in the standard library since C++17.

---

## 1. `std::optional<T>`

```cpp
#include <optional>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// Try to read a user‑supplied number.  If the input is invalid,
// we return an empty optional.
std::optional<int> readNumber(const std::string& text)
{
    try {
        return std::stoi(text);      // succeed → we have a value
    } catch (...) {
        return std::nullopt;         // fail → “no value”
    }
}

int main()
{
    auto maybe = readNumber("42");
    if (maybe) {                     // same as: if (maybe.has_value())
        std::cout << "Got: " << *maybe << '\n';
    } else {
        std::cout << "No valid number\n";
    }

    auto empty = readNumber("abc");
    std::cout << "empty has value? " << std::boolalpha << bool(empty) << '\n';
}
```

### Key points

| Feature | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **Empty state** | `std::nullopt` represents “no value”. The object is *empty* until you assign a value. |
| **Access** | `operator*`, `operator->`, `.value()`. Calling any of them when empty throws `std::bad_optional_access`. |
| **Copy/Move** | `optional<T>` is copyable/movable if `T` is. |
| **No default ctor requirement** | `optional<T>` can be default‑constructed even if `T` is not default‑constructible. |
| **Comparison** | `optional<T>` supports `==`, `!=` against another optional or against `nullopt`. |

> **When to use**  
> Replace “`int*` that may be `nullptr`” or “`T` with a special sentinel value” with `std::optional<T>`. It makes the “missing value” explicit in the type system.

