## 2. `std::variant<T1, T2, …>`

```cpp
#include <variant>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

using Result = std::variant<int, std::string>;   // either a value or an error message

// Parse an integer from a string.  On failure we return an error message.
Result parseInt(const std::string& s)
{
    try {
        return std::stoi(s);            // first alternative (int) is selected
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        return std::string("Parse error: ") + e.what(); // second alternative (string)
    }
}

int main()
{
    std::vector<std::string> inputs{"123", "abc", "9999"};

    for (const auto& in : inputs) {
        Result r = parseInt(in);

        std::visit([](auto&& v) {
            using T = std::decay_t<decltype(v)>;
            if constexpr (std::is_same_v<T, int>)
                std::cout << "Parsed int: " << v << '\n';
            else
                std::cout << "Error: " << v << '\n';
        }, r);
    }

    // Alternatively, we can use holds_alternative / get_if
    Result r2 = "Custom error";
    if (std::holds_alternative<std::string>(r2))
        std::cout << "String alt: " << std::get<std::string>(r2) << '\n';
}
```

### Key points

| Feature | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **Active alternative** | `variant` always contains *exactly one* of its alternatives. The active one is tracked by an index (`.index()`). |
| **Construction** | If you value‑initialise `variant<T1, T2> v;`, the first alternative (`T1`) is default‑constructed. |
| **Access** | `std::get<T>(v)` retrieves the stored value if `T` is the active type (else throws `std::bad_variant_access`). `std::get_if<T>(&v)` returns a pointer or `nullptr`. |
| **Visitors** | `std::visit(visitor, v)` applies a callable that accepts any of the alternative types. It’s the idiomatic way to branch on the active type. |
| **Copy/Move** | `variant` is copyable/movable if all alternatives are. |
| **No common base required** | Alternatives can be completely unrelated types. |

> **When to use**  
> Replace a `union` or a “`enum` + `struct`” pair with `std::variant`. Use it when you need a value that can be one of several distinct types, and you want the compiler to enforce that you handle all possibilities.

